# Part H · Sections 32–33 — aneurysms and dissections; connective tissue disorders of the aorta
Q = [
dict(sec=32, img='H_aaaGross', cap="Autopsy specimen of the abdominal aorta and kidneys",
stem="The abdominal aorta and kidneys of a 74-year-old man who died of an unrelated cause are shown; the asterisk marks the lesion.{FIG}Which modifiable factor most strongly increases the risk of developing this lesion?",
choices=["Diabetes mellitus","High-sodium diet","Hypercholesterolemia","Physical inactivity","Smoking"], key=4,
exp="A fusiform dilation of the aorta <b>below the renal arteries</b> is an <b>infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm</b> (an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is a dilation over 3 cm, or 1.5 times normal). It is the most common aneurysm, degenerative/atherosclerotic, with transmural inflammation, matrix metalloproteinase activity, a thinned media and laminated mural thrombus. Risk factors: <b>smoking (the strongest)</b>, hypertension, age, male sex and family history. Interestingly, <b>diabetes is protective</b>. Complications: rupture, embolization, dissection, fistulization. Always: stop smoking.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Smoking is the strongest risk factor for abdominal aortic aneurysm, which is usually infrarenal.",
wrong={0:"Diabetes is associated with a lower risk of AAA.",
1:"Sodium raises blood pressure but is not the strongest AAA risk factor.",
2:"Cholesterol contributes to atherosclerosis but less than smoking for aneurysm.",
3:"Inactivity is not the main AAA risk factor."},
eli5="Smoking weakens the aortic wall more than anything else, so the belly part of the aorta balloons out."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A 71-year-old man has an asymptomatic infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm. It measured 4.3 cm on ultrasound 6 months ago and 5.0 cm today. He has no abdominal or back pain.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Refer for repair with CT planning","Repeat the ultrasound in 12 months","Repeat ultrasound in 6 months","Start doxycycline to slow its growth","Start a beta-blocker and observe"], key=0,
exp="Abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAAs) grow about 0.2–0.3 cm a year. Indications for repair: <b>symptomatic at any size</b>, <b>diameter over 5.5 cm</b>, <b>growth of more than 5 mm in 6 months</b>, or an eccentric or saccular shape (5.0–5.5 cm in women, familial disease or proven rapid growth). His aneurysm grew <b>7 mm in 6 months</b>, so he should be referred for repair — open or endovascular (EVAR) — with <b>CT angiography</b> (the gold standard) for planning. Smaller aneurysms are watched with ultrasound (Dr. Ciraulo: \"memorize that chart\"); stop smoking and control blood pressure.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> AAA repair is indicated for symptoms, size over 5.5 cm, or growth over 5 mm in 6 months.",
wrong={1:"Rapid growth is an indication for repair, not longer surveillance.",
2:"Rapid growth makes further watchful waiting unsafe.",
3:"Doxycycline has not been shown to slow aneurysm growth.",
4:"No drug reliably slows AAA growth; rapid growth needs repair."},
eli5="It grew 7 millimeters in 6 months. That fast growth means it's getting ready to burst, so it needs fixing even though it isn't huge yet."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_aaaCt', cap="Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen, axial view at L4",
stem="A 77-year-old man has an asymptomatic pulsatile abdominal mass. His CT is shown. An angiogram done at another hospital had reported the aorta as \"only mildly dilated\".{FIG}Which of the following best explains the discrepancy?",
choices=["Angiography overestimates aortic size","CT magnifies vessels by a factor of two","Mural thrombus narrows the visible lumen","The aneurysm shrank between studies","The pulsatile mass is a horseshoe kidney"], key=2,
exp="The CT shows a large aneurysm (about 9 cm, measured to the <b>outer wall</b>). Much of the sac is lined by <b>laminated mural thrombus</b>, so the contrast-filled <b>lumen</b> is much narrower than the aneurysm. Angiography shows only the lumen and therefore <b>underestimates</b> the true size — measure the outer wall on CT (the gold standard; ultrasound for screening and surveillance). A 9-cm aneurysm needs repair; once rupture occurs (back or abdominal pain, pulsatile mass, shock), an unstable patient goes straight to the operating room.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Measure abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAAs) at the outer wall on CT; mural thrombus makes angiography underestimate their size.",
wrong={0:"Angiography underestimates, not overestimates, aneurysm size.",
1:"CT does not magnify vessels.",
3:"Aneurysms do not shrink spontaneously.",
4:"The CT shows an aortic aneurysm, not a kidney."},
eli5="Clot lines the inside of the bulge, so dye on an angiogram only shows the narrow channel through the clot. CT shows the true size of the whole balloon."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A 68-year-old man who smoked a pack a day for 30 years until age 60 comes for a preventive visit. He has no abdominal or back pain and a normal abdominal examination.{FIG}Which screening test for abdominal aortic aneurysm is recommended?",
choices=["Annual abdominal ultrasound for life","CT angiography every 5 years","No screening, because he quit smoking","One-time abdominal ultrasound","Plain abdominal radiograph"], key=3,
exp="The United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommends a <b>one-time abdominal ultrasound</b> in <b>men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked</b> (the lecture slide: men 65–70 and those with a family history). Ultrasound is sensitive, cheap and radiation-free; CT is for surgical planning. A plain film may show an eggshell calcified rim but is not a screening test. Quitting smoking does not remove his eligibility.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Screen men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked with a one-time abdominal ultrasound for abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA).",
wrong={0:"A single normal ultrasound does not need annual repetition.",
1:"CT is for planning repair, not screening.",
2:"\"Ever smoked\" includes former smokers.",
4:"Plain films are insensitive."},
eli5="Any man aged 65 to 75 who has ever smoked gets one belly ultrasound to look for an aortic bulge."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_colitis', cap="Sigmoidoscopic image",
stem="On the night after open repair of an infrarenal aortic aneurysm, a 72-year-old man on a norepinephrine infusion passes bloody diarrhea. His abdomen is mildly distended and tender on the left. Flexible sigmoidoscopy is shown.{FIG}Loss of flow through which artery most likely caused this complication?",
choices=["Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Internal pudendal artery","Renal artery","Superior mesenteric artery"], key=1,
exp="Dusky, ulcerated sigmoid mucosa in the first 12–24 hours after aortic repair, in an unstable patient on vasopressors, is <b>ischemic colitis</b> of the sigmoid, from loss of <b>inferior mesenteric artery</b> flow (often sacrificed during repair) with poor collaterals (marginal artery from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA); the watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid). At surgery, <b>pulsatile back-bleeding</b> from the cut inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) means good SMA collaterals (tie it); a weak trickle means the colon depends on it (re-implant). Confirm with sigmoidoscopy; transmural necrosis needs resection.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Bloody diarrhea after abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair suggests sigmoid ischemic colitis from loss of inferior mesenteric artery flow.",
wrong={0:"The celiac trunk supplies the foregut, not the sigmoid.",
2:"The internal pudendal supplies the perineum.",
3:"Renal artery injury causes kidney failure, not colitis.",
4:"The SMA supplies the midgut; its loss causes small-bowel ischemia."},
eli5="The artery that feeds the lower colon often gets tied off during aneurysm surgery. If the backup supply is weak, that part of the colon starts dying."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_splenic', cap="Contrast-enhanced CT of the upper abdomen",
stem="A 29-year-old woman who hopes to become pregnant within the year has an incidental 2.4-cm splenic artery aneurysm on the CT shown, obtained after a minor car accident. She has no symptoms.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Observation, because it is asymptomatic","Elective repair before pregnancy","Repeat CT after the first trimester","Splenectomy after delivery","Start a beta-blocker and repeat imaging in 2 years"], key=1,
exp="<b>Splenic artery aneurysms</b> are commoner in women and with multiple pregnancies, and they are famous for <b>rupturing in pregnancy</b> (a \"double rupture\" — first into the lesser sac, then free), with high maternal and fetal mortality. Repair (usually endovascular) is indicated if the aneurysm is <b>over 2 cm, symptomatic, or in a woman of child-bearing age</b> — she meets two criteria.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Repair splenic artery aneurysms over 2 cm, symptomatic ones, or any in a woman of child-bearing age, because of rupture risk in pregnancy.",
wrong={0:"Asymptomatic splenic aneurysms in women planning pregnancy still need repair.",
2:"Rupture risk is highest in pregnancy; waiting until then is dangerous.",
3:"Repair should precede pregnancy, not follow it.",
4:"Beta-blockers do not prevent rupture."},
eli5="This artery bulge tends to burst during pregnancy, which can kill mother and baby. Fix it before she gets pregnant."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_popliteal', cap="Angiograms of the left knee region",
stem="A 70-year-old man has a pulsatile mass behind his left knee. Angiography is shown (left), with the result after treatment (right).{FIG}Which additional investigation is most important?",
choices=["Abdominal aortic ultrasound","Echocardiography for a cardiac source of emboli","Nerve conduction studies","Plain film of the knee","Venous duplex for deep venous thrombosis"], key=0,
exp="A <b>popliteal artery aneurysm</b> (shown, then excluded with a stent graft) occurs mainly in men and tends to <b>thrombose or embolize</b> (acute limb ischemia) rather than rupture; repair if over 2 cm or symptomatic. Popliteal aneurysms are <b>bilateral in about half</b> and about <b>a third of patients have an abdominal aortic aneurysm</b>, so image the abdominal aorta (and the other knee).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Popliteal aneurysms embolize or thrombose; screen for a contralateral popliteal aneurysm and an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA).",
wrong={1:"The aneurysm is the embolic source; a cardiac source is not the priority.",
2:"Nerve compression is not the main concern.",
3:"A plain film adds nothing.",
4:"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is not the associated condition to screen for."},
eli5="People with a ballooned artery behind the knee often have one in the other knee and in the belly aorta too. Check the aorta."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A 64-year-old woman develops a tender, pulsatile groin mass with a bruit 2 days after cardiac catheterization through the right femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a sac communicating with the femoral artery through a narrow neck, with to-and-fro flow; the sac wall has no intima or media.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the lesion?",
choices=["Arteriovenous fistula","Dissection of the femoral artery","False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)","Fusiform true aneurysm","Saccular true aneurysm"], key=2,
exp="A <b>false aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> is a breach in the arterial wall — here from the catheter puncture — with an <b>extravascular hematoma</b> that communicates with the lumen and is contained by adventitia and surrounding tissue; the vessel's layers have not dilated. A <b>true aneurysm</b> involves all three layers (saccular, a one-sided outpouching; fusiform, circumferential). A <b>dissection</b> is blood within the media behind an intimal flap. Small femoral pseudoaneurysms can be treated with ultrasound-guided thrombin injection or compression.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A pseudoaneurysm is a contained extravascular hematoma communicating with a breached artery; a true aneurysm involves all three layers.",
wrong={0:"An arteriovenous fistula connects artery and vein with a continuous bruit.",
1:"A dissection tracks within the media.",
3:"A fusiform true aneurysm dilates all three layers circumferentially.",
4:"A saccular true aneurysm still has all three wall layers."},
eli5="The needle hole leaked, and the blood formed a pulsing pocket held in by the surrounding tissue, not by the artery wall itself."),

dict(sec=33, img=None,
stem="A 36-year-old man with long arms and fingers, upward lens dislocation and pectus excavatum dies suddenly. Autopsy shows an ascending aortic dissection. {FIG}Which of the following is most likely on histologic examination of the aortic media?",
choices=["Fragmented elastic fibers with mucoid pools in the media","Granulomas with giant cells and a fragmented internal elastic lamina","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum with plasma cells","Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils","Intimal plaque with cholesterol clefts"], key=0,
exp="Marfan syndrome (FBN1, fibrillin-1) causes <b>cystic medial degeneration</b>: loss and fragmentation of elastic fibers and smooth muscle cells with pools of <b>mucoid (proteoglycan) ground substance</b> in the media. Fibrillin-1 scaffolds elastic fibers and sequesters transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β); loss increases TGF-β signaling. The weakened root dilates (aortic regurgitation) and dissects (usually type A). Cystic medial degeneration is also seen with hypertension and age. Other options: giant cell arteritis; syphilitic aortitis; polyarteritis nodosa; atherosclerosis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Thoracic aneurysm and dissection in Marfan syndrome show cystic medial degeneration.",
wrong={1:"Granulomas with giant cells are giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis.",
2:"Obliterative endarteritis is syphilitic aortitis.",
3:"Fibrinoid necrosis is polyarteritis nodosa or malignant hypertension.",
4:"An atheroma is intimal and causes abdominal more than root aneurysms."},
eli5="In Marfan syndrome the elastic sheets in the aorta break apart and leave gooey gaps, so the wall stretches and tears."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_ctaDissection', cap="Sagittal CT angiogram of the thoracic aorta",
stem="A 63-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension has sudden, severe tearing pain between the shoulder blades. His blood pressure is 196/108 mm Hg in both arms and pulse 102/min. His CT angiogram is shown; the ascending aorta and arch are not involved. He has no limb or organ ischemia.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
choices=["Emergency open surgical repair","Esmolol first, then a vasodilator","Intravenous nitroprusside alone","Oral amlodipine and outpatient follow-up","Thrombolysis"], key=1,
exp="A dissection flap confined to the <b>descending thoracic aorta</b> (distal to the left subclavian) is <b>Stanford B</b> (DeBakey III). Uncomplicated type B is treated <b>medically</b> — \"if you control the hypertension, sometimes these dissections will heal.\" Start with an <b>intravenous beta-blocker</b> (esmolol or labetalol) to a heart rate of about 60 and systolic pressure 100–120 (under 120 within 20 minutes), <b>then</b> add a vasodilator such as nitroprusside if needed; opioids for pain. Complicated type B (malperfusion, rupture, expansion, refractory pain or hypertension) gets thoracic endovascular aortic repair (TEVAR). Never give thrombolytics or anticoagulants.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Uncomplicated Stanford B dissection is treated medically: intravenous beta-blocker first, then vasodilator, to heart rate about 60 and systolic blood pressure 100–120.",
wrong={0:"Emergency open repair is for type A (ascending) dissection.",
2:"A vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia, raising the shear force on the aortic wall.",
3:"An acute dissection needs intravenous titration in intensive care.",
4:"Thrombolysis can be fatal in dissection."},
eli5="The tear is only in the downward part of the aorta. Slow the heart and lower the pressure with intravenous drugs so the wall stops tearing, and it may heal."),

dict(sec=32, img='H_debakey', cap="Schematic of three aortic dissections, numbered 1–3 (false lumen shaded)",
stem="Three patterns of aortic dissection are shown, numbered 1–3.{FIG}Which of them require emergency open surgical repair?",
choices=["1 only","1 and 2","1, 2 and 3","2 and 3","3 only"], key=1,
exp="Dissection 1 starts at the root and extends into the descending aorta (DeBakey I, about 60%); dissection 2 is confined to the ascending aorta (DeBakey II, 10–15%); dissection 3 starts distal to the left subclavian (DeBakey III, 25–30%). <b>Stanford A</b> = <b>any ascending involvement</b> (1 and 2) → <b>emergent open surgical repair</b> whatever the hour (root ± valve, arch with re-implanted head vessels, hypothermic circulatory arrest), because of the risk of rupture into the pericardium (tamponade), acute aortic regurgitation, coronary occlusion and stroke. <b>Stanford B</b> (3) → medical blood-pressure control, thoracic endovascular aortic repair (TEVAR) if complicated.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Stanford type A dissection (any ascending involvement, DeBakey I and II) needs emergency surgery; type B (DeBakey III) is managed medically unless complicated.",
wrong={0:"Dissection 2 also involves the ascending aorta and is type A.",
2:"Dissection 3 (type B) is managed medically unless complicated.",
3:"Dissection 3 is type B; dissection 1 is type A.",
4:"Dissection 3 is the type usually managed medically."},
eli5="If the tear involves the first part of the aorta near the heart, it's a surgical emergency. If it's only in the downward part, start with blood pressure drugs."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A resident starts a nitroprusside infusion in a patient with an acute aortic dissection and a blood pressure of 190/104 mm Hg. The attending stops it and asks for an esmolol infusion first.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the attending's reasoning?",
choices=["Esmolol is a stronger vasodilator than nitroprusside","Vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia","Nitroprusside causes immediate cyanide toxicity","Nitroprusside constricts the vasa vasorum","Nitroprusside is contraindicated with hypertension"], key=1,
exp="\"<b>Impulse control</b>\": the force that propagates a dissection is the rate of rise of aortic pressure (<b>dP/dt</b>, shear stress) as much as the pressure itself. A vasodilator alone triggers <b>reflex tachycardia</b> and more forceful ejection, increasing dP/dt. So an <b>intravenous beta-blocker comes first</b> (esmolol, labetalol), to a heart rate of about 60, and only then a vasodilator to bring the systolic pressure to 100–120.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In aortic dissection, give a beta-blocker before any vasodilator to prevent reflex tachycardia and reduce aortic shear stress.",
wrong={0:"Esmolol is a beta-blocker, not a vasodilator.",
2:"Cyanide toxicity occurs with prolonged high-dose infusion, not immediately.",
3:"Nitroprusside dilates, not constricts, vessels.",
4:"Nitroprusside is used for hypertensive emergencies, after beta-blockade in dissection."},
eli5="Dropping the pressure without slowing the heart makes the heart race and pound harder, which pushes the tear further. Slow the heart first."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A 48-year-old security guard collapses with right-sided weakness and aphasia. He also complained of chest pain radiating to his back. Blood pressure is 150/90 mm Hg in the right arm and 108/70 mm Hg in the left. The stroke team is preparing intravenous alteplase.{FIG}Which of the following should be done before thrombolysis?",
choices=["Carotid duplex ultrasound only","CT angiography of the chest and neck","Lumbar puncture","Proceed with alteplase without delay","Transcranial Doppler"], key=1,
exp="A <b>type A aortic dissection can present as a stroke</b> when the flap occludes a carotid artery (Dr. McGrath's 48-year-old security guard, whose stroke CT angiogram found a dissection occluding the left carotid). Chest or back pain, an <b>arm blood-pressure difference over 20 mm Hg</b>, a wide mediastinum or a new aortic regurgitation murmur must prompt <b>CT angiography of the chest</b> (and neck) before thrombolysis or anticoagulation, which can be fatal in dissection. \"It's not time to break out the manipulation box. That's the time to get a CAT scan.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Exclude aortic dissection (chest/back pain, arm pressure difference) with CT angiography before giving thrombolytics for stroke.",
wrong={0:"Carotid ultrasound alone would miss the aortic dissection.",
2:"Lumbar puncture is not indicated.",
3:"Thrombolysis in an unrecognized dissection can cause fatal bleeding.",
4:"Transcranial Doppler does not image the aorta."},
eli5="A tear in the aorta can block a neck artery and look like an ordinary stroke. Clot-busting drugs would make the tear bleed. Scan the chest first."),

dict(sec=32, img=None,
stem="A 66-year-old woman undergoes open repair of a descending thoracic aortic aneurysm with aortic cross-clamping. When she wakes, she cannot move her legs, and pain and temperature sensation are lost below the umbilicus while vibration and position sense are preserved.{FIG}Loss of flow through which artery most likely caused this deficit?",
choices=["Anterior spinal artery from the vertebral arteries","Artery of Adamkiewicz","Inferior mesenteric artery","Internal iliac artery","Posterior spinal arteries"], key=1,
exp="Paraplegia with loss of pain and temperature but <b>preserved dorsal columns</b> is an <b>anterior spinal cord syndrome</b>. Below the mid-thoracic cord, the anterior spinal artery is reinforced by the <b>artery of Adamkiewicz</b> (great radicular artery), usually arising from an intercostal or lumbar artery between <b>T9 and T12</b> (the recording said T4–T8). Clamping or covering it during descending aortic repair can infarct the cord. Prevention: shorter clamp times or distal aortic perfusion, and a <b>lumbar cerebrospinal fluid drain</b> to improve cord perfusion pressure.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Paraplegia after descending aortic repair results from loss of the artery of Adamkiewicz (anterior spinal cord syndrome).",
wrong={0:"The vertebral contribution supplies the cervical cord, not the thoracolumbar cord.",
2:"The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) supplies the colon.",
3:"The internal iliac supplies the pelvis.",
4:"Posterior spinal artery loss would affect the dorsal columns, which are preserved."},
eli5="One key artery coming off the aorta feeds the lower spinal cord. Clamping the aorta above it starved the cord, so her legs stopped working."),

dict(sec=33, img=None,
stem="A 19-year-old man with Marfan syndrome (FBN1 mutation) has an aortic root diameter of 4.2 cm, increased from 3.9 cm a year ago. He plays competitive basketball and lifts heavy weights.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management now?",
choices=["Losartan or beta-blocker; serial imaging","Immediate aortic root replacement","Long-term fluoroquinolone prophylaxis","No change; imaging only if symptoms develop","Stop all exercise and start warfarin"], key=0,
exp="Marfan management: <b>regular imaging</b> of the aortic root and ascending aorta (echocardiography, then magnetic resonance or CT angiography) and screening of first-degree relatives; <b>slow dilation</b> with a <b>beta-blocker</b> (less shear stress) and/or an <b>angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) such as losartan</b> (also damps transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β) signaling); <b>avoid contact sports and heavy isometric lifting</b>; avoid fluoroquinolones; plan pregnancy carefully. <b>Prophylactic root replacement</b> (often valve-sparing) is done at about <b>5.0 cm</b>, earlier with rapid growth (over about 0.5 cm a year) or a family history of dissection, and at smaller sizes in Loeys–Dietz.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Marfan aortopathy: beta-blocker/ARB, avoid contact and isometric exercise, serial imaging, prophylactic root replacement around 5.0 cm.",
wrong={1:"At 4.2 cm with moderate growth, surgery is not yet indicated.",
2:"Fluoroquinolones are avoided because they may increase aneurysm and dissection risk.",
3:"Marfan patients need routine surveillance, not symptom-triggered imaging.",
4:"Anticoagulation has no role, and moderate aerobic exercise is encouraged."},
eli5="His aorta is stretching. Medicine slows it, heavy lifting and contact sports speed it up, and surgery replaces the root once it reaches about 5 cm."),

dict(sec=33, img='H_ehlers', cap="Facial photographs of two patients with the same inherited disorder",
stem="A 24-year-old woman has thin, translucent skin with visible veins on her chest, extensive bruising with minor trauma, and the facial features shown. She has less joint hypermobility than her friends with \"double-jointed\" fingers. Her brother died at 28 of a spontaneous colon perforation.{FIG}Which of the following best describes her disorder?",
choices=["COL3A1 mutation with risk of arterial, bowel and uterine rupture","FBN1 mutation with lens dislocation and aortic root dilation","TGFBR mutation with bifid uvula and hypertelorism","45,X with coarctation and bicuspid valve","Mutation of lysyl hydroxylase with severe joint laxity and scoliosis"], key=0,
exp="Thin, translucent skin with visible veins, easy bruising, a characteristic face (thin nose and lips, prominent eyes, hollow cheeks), relatively little joint laxity, and spontaneous rupture of hollow organs in a relative is <b>vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (type IV)</b>: autosomal dominant <b>COL3A1</b> mutation (collagen III, which gives arteries and hollow organs tensile strength). Medium arteries, bowel and uterus can <b>rupture without warning</b>; arterial dissection and carotid–cavernous fistula occur. <b>Avoid invasive procedures</b> where possible — even angiography can tear vessels.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (EDS) (COL3A1) causes spontaneous rupture of arteries, bowel and uterus; avoid invasive procedures.",
wrong={1:"FBN1 is Marfan syndrome, with tall stature and lens dislocation upward.",
2:"TGFBR mutations cause Loeys–Dietz syndrome.",
3:"Turner syndrome is 45,X with coarctation and bicuspid valve.",
4:"Lysyl hydroxylase deficiency causes kyphoscoliotic EDS with marked joint laxity."},
eli5="A faulty collagen III makes thin skin and fragile arteries and bowel, which can tear apart on their own."),

dict(sec=33, img=None,
stem="A 14-year-old girl has hypertelorism, a bifid uvula, club foot and widespread arterial tortuosity on imaging. Her aortic root measures 3.9 cm, and her mother had an aortic dissection at a root diameter of 4.2 cm.{FIG}Mutation of which pathway is most likely, and what does it imply for management?",
choices=["Collagen III; avoid all imaging","Fibrillin-1; replace the root at 6 cm","TGF-β receptor; operate at smaller sizes","Elastin; supravalvular stenosis surgery","Endoglin; embolize arteriovenous malformations"], key=2,
exp="Hypertelorism, <b>bifid uvula or cleft palate</b>, club foot and <b>arterial tortuosity</b> with aneurysms throughout (including head and neck) is <b>Loeys–Dietz syndrome</b>: autosomal dominant mutations in <b>TGF-β pathway genes (TGFBR1/2</b>, SMAD3, TGFB2). Aortic disease is <b>aggressive — dissection occurs at smaller diameters</b> than in Marfan, so prophylactic root replacement is offered earlier (around 4.0–4.5 cm). Whole-body arterial imaging is needed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Loeys–Dietz (TGF-β receptor) syndrome: bifid uvula, hypertelorism, arterial tortuosity, and aggressive aortic disease needing earlier root replacement.",
wrong={0:"Collagen III defects are vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (EDS); imaging (non-invasive) is still used.",
1:"FBN1 is Marfan, and 6 cm would be far too late for any aortopathy.",
3:"Elastin deletion is Williams syndrome.",
4:"Endoglin mutations cause hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia."},
eli5="A faulty growth-signal receptor makes twisty, weak arteries that tear even when they aren't very wide. The aorta has to be fixed earlier."),
]
